\newlabel{tab:capabilities}{{1}{2}{Capability comparison. Email is the model web10 generalizes, not a social system. The entries summarize the models discussed in the cited work, not an exhaustive survey of implementations. web10's column describes its architecture; production cross-node integration remains incomplete.\relax }{table.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces System overview: one client, two sovereign nodes, one access language. Node A holds the reader's home account and grants; node B is foreign. Cross-node reads are client-mediated: the client queries each node directly with the home-node token, and each node re-authorizes every read against its own groups and grants. Node B asks A's issuer once over HTTPS \texttt  {/certify} and caches the identity answer; remote data is content, never control---a foreign response is never query input, never a key into the local store, and never a grant (\S  \ref  {sec:network}).\relax }}{2}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:overview}{{1}{2}{System overview: one client, two sovereign nodes, one access language. Node A holds the reader's home account and grants; node B is foreign. Cross-node reads are client-mediated: the client queries each node directly with the home-node token, and each node re-authorizes every read against its own groups and grants. Node B asks A's issuer once over HTTPS \texttt {/certify} and caches the identity answer; remote data is content, never control---a foreign response is never query input, never a key into the local store, and never a grant (\S \ref {sec:network}).\relax }{figure.caption.4}{}}
