\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces web10 vs Solid: where the composition happens---the same feed, composed in two different places. Left: Solid's one-user-per-pod store: a cross-user read pulls every pod the query touches, and a discover-type query must pull the whole network, so it does not terminate at scale. Right: three web10 nodes, each pooling millions of users in one shared ClickHouse store; discover, trending, and ranking are ordinary queries the application writes against it, each node re-checking the reader's rules on every read. The strip names the win: composition---a cross-user query that terminates (\S  \ref  {sec:enforcement}, \S  \ref  {sec:related}).\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:composition}{{2}{5}{web10 vs Solid: where the composition happens---the same feed, composed in two different places. Left: Solid's one-user-per-pod store: a cross-user read pulls every pod the query touches, and a discover-type query must pull the whole network, so it does not terminate at scale. Right: three web10 nodes, each pooling millions of users in one shared ClickHouse store; discover, trending, and ranking are ordinary queries the application writes against it, each node re-checking the reader's rules on every read. The strip names the win: composition---a cross-user query that terminates (\S \ref {sec:enforcement}, \S \ref {sec:related}).\relax }{figure.caption.5}{}}
