\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The web10-social application and its node---the data layer in the browser, a generic node, and its two stores, with the messages that move between them. The browser frame holds the application's surfaces and the six data-layer modules a bespoke API would normally carry: feed and ranking (the app's own queries: joins, scoring, cursors), group discovery (the social graph as group rows the app reads and writes), faces and avatars, media resolution, request orchestration (batching, dedup, optimistic writes, retry), and acting identity (scoped, expiring tokens--- what acts as whom)---plus the SDK. The node frame is thin and generic: authentication, the read gate, the query engine, and media (signed URLs to object storage; the render pipeline to HLS renditions). The stores are drawn with their real names: ClickHouse's \texttt  {documents} and group tables, and the object store's media keys. The messages panel shows the payloads that move: \texttt  {sql + token} down; the compiled query---not the caller's string---and rows with signed URLs back; then the signed URL, the GET, and the bytes. Pinned: 46,285 lines of application-local source across 141 files (\S  \ref  {sec:platform:frontend}).\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:app-arch}{{4}{9}{The web10-social application and its node---the data layer in the browser, a generic node, and its two stores, with the messages that move between them. The browser frame holds the application's surfaces and the six data-layer modules a bespoke API would normally carry: feed and ranking (the app's own queries: joins, scoring, cursors), group discovery (the social graph as group rows the app reads and writes), faces and avatars, media resolution, request orchestration (batching, dedup, optimistic writes, retry), and acting identity (scoped, expiring tokens--- what acts as whom)---plus the SDK. The node frame is thin and generic: authentication, the read gate, the query engine, and media (signed URLs to object storage; the render pipeline to HLS renditions). The stores are drawn with their real names: ClickHouse's \texttt {documents} and group tables, and the object store's media keys. The messages panel shows the payloads that move: \texttt {sql + token} down; the compiled query---not the caller's string---and rows with signed URLs back; then the signed URL, the GET, and the bytes. Pinned: 46,285 lines of application-local source across 141 files (\S \ref {sec:platform:frontend}).\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
