\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Pulling the web10 feed across nodes. The reader issues reads with its home-node token; when a read lands on foreign node B, B authenticates the token over the issuer's HTTPS \texttt  {/certify}---one cached answer per token, never a grant---and then runs the same access check node A runs on local reads: contracts and membership against its own store, on every read. One node implementation, two sovereign stores; remote data is content, never control (\S  \ref  {sec:network}).\relax }}{10}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:feed}{{5}{10}{Pulling the web10 feed across nodes. The reader issues reads with its home-node token; when a read lands on foreign node B, B authenticates the token over the issuer's HTTPS \texttt {/certify}---one cached answer per token, never a grant---and then runs the same access check node A runs on local reads: contracts and membership against its own store, on every read. One node implementation, two sovereign stores; remote data is content, never control (\S \ref {sec:network}).\relax }{figure.caption.15}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces A group---``a shared space on your node.'' Membership, roles, and per-post visibility (Public / node / group) expose application choices over the generic groups and grants of \S  \ref  {sec:model}.\relax }}{10}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:groups}{{6}{10}{A group---``a shared space on your node.'' Membership, roles, and per-post visibility (Public / node / group) expose application choices over the generic groups and grants of \S \ref {sec:model}.\relax }{figure.caption.16}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces web10 vs Mastodon / Bluesky: the interoperability fork---where a second app's read of the user's data lands and runs. Left: the fixed social object model (post $\cdot $ profile $\cdot $ follow $\cdot $ like $\cdot $ boost); there is no query engine in the platform---the app reads through the platform's fixed endpoints (untunable) or replicates records into its own database (Bluesky's own statusphere tutorial stands up a sync worker and a separate store just to have a feed)---and the model has no record shape for a hi-score or a listing. Right: the web10 node, set up already: the social app's services (\texttt  {posts} $\cdot $ \texttt  {comments} $\cdot $ \texttt  {reactions} $\cdot $ \texttt  {profile} $\cdot $ \texttt  {media} $\cdot $ \texttt  {music} $\cdot $ \texttt  {groups} $\cdot $ \ldots  ; \texttt  {profile} highlighted---the one the interop grant covers) plus the new apps' own (\texttt  {snake\_scores} $\cdot $ \texttt  {marketplace\_listings}); the API in action---the query engine (\texttt  {POST /v3/query}) compiles the app's query against the reader's grants, then runs it---over the ClickHouse columnar store, provisioned on the node, no database to stand up; a second app steps in through a per-service grant the user gives, and no grant, no read. The strip scores the matchup: web10---the interoperability winner (an app-to-app per-service grant on one shared columnar store; a new read pattern is a new query, not a new backend).\relax }}{11}{figure.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:platforms}{{7}{11}{web10 vs Mastodon / Bluesky: the interoperability fork---where a second app's read of the user's data lands and runs. Left: the fixed social object model (post $\cdot $ profile $\cdot $ follow $\cdot $ like $\cdot $ boost); there is no query engine in the platform---the app reads through the platform's fixed endpoints (untunable) or replicates records into its own database (Bluesky's own statusphere tutorial stands up a sync worker and a separate store just to have a feed)---and the model has no record shape for a hi-score or a listing. Right: the web10 node, set up already: the social app's services (\texttt {posts} $\cdot $ \texttt {comments} $\cdot $ \texttt {reactions} $\cdot $ \texttt {profile} $\cdot $ \texttt {media} $\cdot $ \texttt {music} $\cdot $ \texttt {groups} $\cdot $ \ldots ; \texttt {profile} highlighted---the one the interop grant covers) plus the new apps' own (\texttt {snake\_scores} $\cdot $ \texttt {marketplace\_listings}); the API in action---the query engine (\texttt {POST /v3/query}) compiles the app's query against the reader's grants, then runs it---over the ClickHouse columnar store, provisioned on the node, no database to stand up; a second app steps in through a per-service grant the user gives, and no grant, no read. The strip scores the matchup: web10---the interoperability winner (an app-to-app per-service grant on one shared columnar store; a new read pattern is a new query, not a new backend).\relax }{figure.caption.17}{}}
