\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces How money flows on web10---the ad rail. Top: every application that writes into the store inherits the rail---web10-social builds the catalog today; the snake game and the marketplace inherit it (a new ad surface is a new query, not a new backend). Middle: the node's rail---the boundary attaches the ads, checked the same way every other read is checked, with the density split explicit: the creator's layer dense (the influencer's own offers on roughly 80--90\% of their content) and the node's layer sparse (the operator's overlay on roughly 5--10\% of posts, node-controlled, default 10, deterministic per reader); both can be present on the same post, and the node's overlay never steals the creator's monetization. The inventory is tagged documents plus the offer object---kind, the link that pays, call to action, disclosure---never rewritten, never cloaked. Bottom: the money, off-platform---the creator keeps 100\% of affiliate and sponsor revenue (no cut of ad revenue, no payment processed); the node operator is a media company who sells the attention directly and keeps roughly 85--90\%, and nodes compete for the influencers by lowering their rates. The separate layer names the horizon (the exchange, the payments rail, the creator's own layout)---not this system's claim. The amber strip: Solid / Mastodon / Bluesky ship no ad mechanism---a documented design property; web10's answer: the free web should have ads.\relax }}{13}{figure.caption.20}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:monetization}{{9}{13}{How money flows on web10---the ad rail. Top: every application that writes into the store inherits the rail---web10-social builds the catalog today; the snake game and the marketplace inherit it (a new ad surface is a new query, not a new backend). Middle: the node's rail---the boundary attaches the ads, checked the same way every other read is checked, with the density split explicit: the creator's layer dense (the influencer's own offers on roughly 80--90\% of their content) and the node's layer sparse (the operator's overlay on roughly 5--10\% of posts, node-controlled, default 10, deterministic per reader); both can be present on the same post, and the node's overlay never steals the creator's monetization. The inventory is tagged documents plus the offer object---kind, the link that pays, call to action, disclosure---never rewritten, never cloaked. Bottom: the money, off-platform---the creator keeps 100\% of affiliate and sponsor revenue (no cut of ad revenue, no payment processed); the node operator is a media company who sells the attention directly and keeps roughly 85--90\%, and nodes compete for the influencers by lowering their rates. The separate layer names the horizon (the exchange, the payments rail, the creator's own layout)---not this system's claim. The amber strip: Solid / Mastodon / Bluesky ship no ad mechanism---a documented design property; web10's answer: the free web should have ads.\relax }{figure.caption.20}{}}
