\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces The audience travels with the domain: one influencer, two nodes, the DNS flip---the move of Figure\nonbreakingspace \ref  {fig:domain-follow} drawn as the mechanism. The fans: each follow is the fan's own data---a private document in the fan's store whose body is exactly the domain; one follow write, unchanged when the creator moves; the fan's application re-resolves the address on each read. The domain chip: \texttt  {jacobhoffman.xyz}---the address the audience knows; the node and username are the replaceable target. The DNS card, shown in both states: before \texttt  {provider=node-a.example}, now \texttt  {provider=node-b.example}---one field changed. A TXT record, not an A or CNAME record: an identity claim, the same pattern as DKIM and SPF; the record lives on the creator's DNS, and the node controls no part of it. A missing or ambiguous record gives an explicit unavailable state---the follow stays, and restoring the record recovers it without refollowing. Node A (faded): the home the record used to name; what it stored is a copy of the alias. Node B (active): the verify gate---the node does the DNS lookup itself, the record must name this node and this account, discovery not authentication; the content is served under the same address, and the fans' unchanged documents now resolve here. One DNS change, not a migration (\S  \ref  {sec:network}).\relax }}{17}{figure.caption.22}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:audience-portability}{{11}{17}{The audience travels with the domain: one influencer, two nodes, the DNS flip---the move of Figure~\ref {fig:domain-follow} drawn as the mechanism. The fans: each follow is the fan's own data---a private document in the fan's store whose body is exactly the domain; one follow write, unchanged when the creator moves; the fan's application re-resolves the address on each read. The domain chip: \texttt {jacobhoffman.xyz}---the address the audience knows; the node and username are the replaceable target. The DNS card, shown in both states: before \texttt {provider=node-a.example}, now \texttt {provider=node-b.example}---one field changed. A TXT record, not an A or CNAME record: an identity claim, the same pattern as DKIM and SPF; the record lives on the creator's DNS, and the node controls no part of it. A missing or ambiguous record gives an explicit unavailable state---the follow stays, and restoring the record recovers it without refollowing. Node A (faded): the home the record used to name; what it stored is a copy of the alias. Node B (active): the verify gate---the node does the DNS lookup itself, the record must name this node and this account, discovery not authentication; the content is served under the same address, and the fans' unchanged documents now resolve here. One DNS change, not a migration (\S \ref {sec:network}).\relax }{figure.caption.22}{}}
