\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Where the access layer's cost lives: readable-group resolution time versus the number of candidate groups $K$ (log-log), the same trial as Table\nonbreakingspace \ref  {tab:fanout}. The per-group resolver (amber) grows $\times $100.44 over $K = 10 \rightarrow 1{,}000$ and reaches 7.64\,s at $K = 1{,}000$; the batched resolver (green) grows $\times $9.12 and sits at 214\,ms readable (363\,ms handler). $K = 1$ is the discover board---one candidate group, an open marker on the dashed leg---where the per-group path is faster, because three fixed round trips dominate. The knee is the tradeoff; the batched resolver is the design decision the measurement drove (\S  \ref  {sec:lessons}).\relax }}{18}{figure.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:k1000-curve}{{12}{18}{Where the access layer's cost lives: readable-group resolution time versus the number of candidate groups $K$ (log-log), the same trial as Table~\ref {tab:fanout}. The per-group resolver (amber) grows $\times $100.44 over $K = 10 \rightarrow 1{,}000$ and reaches 7.64\,s at $K = 1{,}000$; the batched resolver (green) grows $\times $9.12 and sits at 214\,ms readable (363\,ms handler). $K = 1$ is the discover board---one candidate group, an open marker on the dashed leg---where the per-group path is faster, because three fixed round trips dominate. The knee is the tradeoff; the batched resolver is the design decision the measurement drove (\S \ref {sec:lessons}).\relax }{figure.caption.24}{}}
