\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces The competitive node economy: a matching graph, 6 $\times $ 6. Six influencers (left, neutral) matched against six nodes (right, green), each vertex carrying the market properties that decide the pick. The nodes price their own terms: A bans the least, the higher price $\cdot $ B a certain political match, mid price $\cdot $ C the lowest price, the heavy overlay $\cdot $ D the overlay cut to 5\%, mid price $\cdot $ E community-governed moderation, mid price $\cdot $ F the self-hosted floor--- free, never gated, the isolated outside option that keeps the market's prices honest. The influencers carry their own properties (biggest audience / delivery-first $\cdot $ price-first $\cdot $ audience shifted political $\cdot $ the shopper $\cdot $ growing, was price-first $\cdot $ the community audience) with the fan dots by each: the follow is the fan's own data, bound to the domain---a flip is a rematch, not a loss. The edges carry the dynamics: the stable matches; two flips---one because the preference changed, one because the node acted; the shopping leg; and node C losing two of its three, because price alone does not hold the audience. The amber strip: the three rents the paved platforms hold over the creator---reach throttling, demonetization, deplatforming---structurally out; so the competition is on terms, not on leverage (\S  \ref  {sec:platform:ads}).\relax }}{20}{figure.caption.25}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:node-market}{{13}{20}{The competitive node economy: a matching graph, 6 $\times $ 6. Six influencers (left, neutral) matched against six nodes (right, green), each vertex carrying the market properties that decide the pick. The nodes price their own terms: A bans the least, the higher price $\cdot $ B a certain political match, mid price $\cdot $ C the lowest price, the heavy overlay $\cdot $ D the overlay cut to 5\%, mid price $\cdot $ E community-governed moderation, mid price $\cdot $ F the self-hosted floor--- free, never gated, the isolated outside option that keeps the market's prices honest. The influencers carry their own properties (biggest audience / delivery-first $\cdot $ price-first $\cdot $ audience shifted political $\cdot $ the shopper $\cdot $ growing, was price-first $\cdot $ the community audience) with the fan dots by each: the follow is the fan's own data, bound to the domain---a flip is a rematch, not a loss. The edges carry the dynamics: the stable matches; two flips---one because the preference changed, one because the node acted; the shopping leg; and node C losing two of its three, because price alone does not hold the audience. The amber strip: the three rents the paved platforms hold over the creator---reach throttling, demonetization, deplatforming---structurally out; so the competition is on terms, not on leverage (\S \ref {sec:platform:ads}).\relax }{figure.caption.25}{}}
